---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePages } from '../data/site-pages';
import themes from '../data/themes.json';
---
<Layout {...sitePages.features}>
  <section class="wrap guide-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Features / Eitr Linux</p>
    <h1>One desktop.<br /><em>Many ways to use it.</em></h1>
    <p class="lead">Eitr Linux brings an Arch-based system, a native Hyprland desktop and everyday tools into one distribution. Here is what is available, what is optional, and what still needs testing.</p>
    <nav class="info-links" aria-label="Feature sections">
      <a class="text-link" href="#desktop">Desktop</a><a class="text-link" href="#development">Development &amp; AI</a><a class="text-link" href="#accounts">Accounts</a><a class="text-link" href="#organizations">Organizations</a><a class="text-link" href="#recovery">Recovery</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
  <div class="wrap info-body">
    <section class="info-section" id="desktop" aria-labelledby="features-desktop">
      <p class="eyebrow">Desktop &amp; appearance</p>
      <h2 id="features-desktop">Keyboard-first.<br /><em>Graphical when you need it.</em></h2>
      <p>The custom Quickshell desktop includes native notifications, a workspace Overview with live previews, and panels for audio, WiFi, Bluetooth, displays, calendar, weather and hardware. The Eitr logo or Super + comma opens Control Center, including a disposable, read-only quick chat that knows Eitr through the Handbook. Walker searches applications, menus, settings and actions. Install &gt; VPN offers Tailscale, WireGuard configuration import and NetworkManager VPN plugins, with reviewed imports and explicit connection choices.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Choose Full, Centered with evenly packed icons around a centered clock, or Tabs: three content-sized tabs joined by slopes on a thin strip, floating panels and weather on hover. Active workspace glyphs use the accent color; AI Usage has its own icon.</li>
        <li>Place monitors beside one another, choose a main display, and show the bar on one or all screens. HDR monitors get an HDR switch with SDR content brightness.</li>
        <li>Adjust per-application audio and microphone controls in one panel; choose Bars or Line for the media equalizer.</li>
        <li>Choose from {themes.length} themes, with matching application colors, lockscreens, 4K wallpapers and ultrawide artwork. Eitr is the default; it and each realm have their own Neovim and VS Code colors, and setup wizards follow the current theme.</li>
        <li>Add wallpapers from a file or link for all themes or one theme; generate a theme from a picture, edit colors live, and create your own lockscreen.</li>
        <li>Keep your preferred bar position, corners, cursor and fonts when switching themes. Auto-hide reveals the bar at its screen edge; Reduce motion makes desktop transitions instant.</li>
      </ul>
      <a class="text-link" href="/#tour">See the real desktop →</a>
    </section>
    <section class="info-section" id="development" aria-labelledby="features-development">
      <p class="eyebrow">Development &amp; AI</p>
      <h2 id="features-development">Tools for the work.<br /><em>Assistance by choice.</em></h2>
      <p>Kitty, Zsh, tmux, Neovim with LazyVim, Docker, Lazygit and Lazydocker form the development workspace. Menus offer additional editors, language environments, database clients, API tools and monitoring dashboards. Schema v2 plugins add tools, tiles, panels, desktop widgets and bar items from reviewed Git commits. Control Center’s Plugins page manages them; <a href="/development/#plugins-title">Update Plugins</a> reviews updates separately from System Update.</p>
      <p>Claude Code, Codex, GitHub Copilot CLI, Grok and Gemini CLI are available through first-use installation. Antigravity, OpenCode and Pi are also available as standalone assistants. Super + A opens your default assistant trusted in the home folder, with full access and offline Handbook guidance where supported. System &gt; Security &gt; Assistant Access restores permission prompts. Development &gt; AI opens normal sessions in a picked folder, with account switching and session options where supported. Ratatoskr is the experimental offline assistant. The bar shows supported usage information.</p>
      <p>The optional “Let assistants work together” feature connects supported clients through a local bridge. Another signed-in assistant can give a read-only second opinion from the same working folder. Hand-offs retain the caller’s tool-approval boundary; helpers cannot delegate onward. Real-account verification remains a separate step from mock and unit tests; Gemini has answered read-only and through the bridge with a free API key; Antigravity is not a collaboration helper.</p>
      <p>Failed task terminals offer Ask the AI; crash notifications offer assistant help or a problem report with crash details. These are opt-in workflows; Eitr does not silently send every crash to an AI service. Provider accounts, subscriptions and network access are separate from the distribution.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/development/">Development workflows, defaults &amp; optional tools →</a>
    </section>
    <section class="info-section" id="accounts" aria-labelledby="features-accounts">
      <p class="eyebrow">Accounts, cloud &amp; Git</p>
      <h2 id="features-accounts">Know the account.<br /><em>Keep the context visible.</em></h2>
      <p>Git Accounts manages folder-specific identities and SSH keys. Cloud menus make provider accounts and project, subscription, region or scope selections explicit, with navigation back through account, service and resource menus.</p>
      <p>System &gt; Security &gt; Users lists existing human accounts as read-only rows. Create User… opens a wizard for the username, optional full name, Standard or Administrator account type and password, with a review before creation. Each new account sets up its own desktop at its first Hyprland login through UWSM, without copying files from your account. User deletion is not offered.</p>
      <p>Provider menus cover AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, Cloudflare, DigitalOcean, Hetzner Cloud, IBM Cloud, Netlify, Oracle Cloud and Vercel. The newer provider workflows have isolated account workspaces and mock-tested resource views; live installation, sign-in and remote context checks remain open where documented. Menu availability is not a claim that every vendor workflow has passed real-account testing.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="info-section" id="organizations" aria-labelledby="features-organizations">
      <p class="eyebrow">Organization profiles</p>
      <h2 id="features-organizations">Set it up once.<br /><em>Install it on every desk.</em></h2>
      <p>An organization profile is a JSON file on the installer’s answers stick. It removes reviewed optional apps, adds packages, Flathub apps and checksummed package files, sets defaults such as the theme, wallpaper, browser and an extra signed repository, and applies policies: leave out groups such as gaming or media, and hide and refuse remote access, assistant collaboration and update-channel changes. Review lists every change before the disk is touched; <code>eitr profile check</code> and <code>eitr profile apply</code> do the same on an installed machine.</p>
      <p>Policies govern Eitr’s own tools; they are not a lockdown. A user with root can edit or override them, so real lockdown also means not granting root. Installs with and without a network, a refused profile and applying a profile to an installed machine have passed in a virtual machine. Package upgrades, hidden controls and installer screens in every language have also passed in a virtual machine.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/eitr-org/eitr/blob/main/distro/ORGANIZATION-PROFILES.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the profile format and limits ↗</a>
    </section>
    <section class="info-section" aria-labelledby="features-everyday">
      <p class="eyebrow">Everyday tools &amp; gaming</p>
      <h2 id="features-everyday">Capture an idea.<br /><em>Make room to play.</em></h2>
      <p>Capture screenshots, record with OBS, read screen text or QR codes, pick a color and share nearby with LocalSend. Tap Super + D twice or hold and release for local dictation. Hold Super + Shift + D for voice reminders, or type everyday times such as “9am”, “in 5 minutes” and “every 29th”. Repeats and appointment alerts appear in the calendar; a missing time opens the native HH:MM clock with any assistant. Alerts chime unless Do Not Disturb is on, with snooze and dismiss actions in popups and notification history.</p>
      <p>Tools &gt; Calendars adds read-only Google, Outlook or iCal subscriptions using a secret calendar address, refreshed when you open the panel. Edit provider events at their source. The offline Handbook opens with Super + F1.</p>
      <p>Web apps open in their own window with a chosen browser profile. Eitr follows your compatible default browser, supports Vivaldi and offers to install Chromium when needed.</p>
      <p>Steam comes installed with GameMode, MangoHud and gamescope; RetroArch and GeForce NOW are optional, for classic and cloud gaming. On HDR monitors, fullscreen HDR games turn HDR on by themselves, tone-mapped to the monitor's own brightness range, and an HDR switch in the display settings shows the whole desktop in HDR. Game Mode can disable animations, blur and notification popups. Individual games, controllers and cloud services have their own compatibility and account requirements.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="info-section" id="recovery" aria-labelledby="features-recovery">
      <p class="eyebrow">Maintenance &amp; recovery</p>
      <h2 id="features-recovery">A way forward.<br /><em>A way back.</em></h2>
      <p>System Update checks Arch news and available changes, creates a Btrfs snapshot when an upgrade is needed, and updates from Arch and Eitr’s signed repository, repairing stale package-list signatures when needed. Installing an app while updates are waiting explains why the system must be current first and offers to run System Update, snapshot included, then install. From a terminal, <code>eitr update pacman</code>, <code>eitr update aur</code> and <code>eitr update full</code> update without automatic snapshots. While it works, each step shows a turning rune and Bifröst-colored text, and pacman shows color and its Pac-Man progress bar.</p>
      <p>System &gt; Maintenance &gt; Check this PC runs read-only checks with suggested fixes. Optional deeper checks ask for your password; neither repairs nor restarts anything.</p>
      <p>System &gt; Maintenance &gt; Choose fastest mirrors uses reflector to rank Arch package mirrors by download speed. Fresh installs try automatically after a boot with a network, with up to three connected attempts until ranking succeeds. If ranking fails, the existing list, including custom mirrors, stays unchanged.</p>
      <p>Recovery includes system snapshots, a boot guard, optional home-folder snapshots and factory-reset procedures. The Handbook describes supported layouts, destructive confirmations and limitations. Snapshots are not a substitute for an independent backup. Both factory resets and the boot guard’s rollback have passed on an encrypted disk in a virtual machine.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/guide/recovery/">Read the recovery Handbook →</a>
    </section>
    <section class="info-section" aria-labelledby="features-remote">
      <p class="eyebrow">Remote access / Explicit setup</p>
      <h2 id="features-remote">Remote access.<br /><em>Share by choice.</em></h2>
      <p>Moonlight, Sunshine sharing and Tailscale away access have setup workflows and native status panels. Sharing remains inactive until explicitly configured. It controls a signed-in, awake desktop. Real-device checks are recorded, but final validation remains open; headless use, access before login and wake on LAN are future work. Stop sharing before locking when privacy matters.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/eitr-org/eitr/blob/main/distro/REMOTE-ACCESS.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the setup and security boundaries ↗</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
